.TH SIGNALTEST 1
.SH NAME
signaltest \- check signal(2) against the definitions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B signaltest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Signaltest
checks
.IR signal (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number as well.
.PP
The transform is checked against the
.RI O( n \u\s-22\s+2\d)
sum it is an accelerated form of, at seventeen lengths, powers of two
and otherwise.
That is an oracle and not a property: if the two disagree the fast one
is wrong, and no argument about which is more likely applies.
The lengths that are not powers of two go through Bluestein's method,
which is a different piece of code that nothing else here would reach.
.PP
Everything else is checked against a closed form too: Parseval, the
shift theorem, the convolution theorem, the exact height a sinusoid must
reach in a periodogram under any window, and \- for a Butterworth filter
\- the whole of its magnitude response, not merely the \-3 dB point.
The bilinear transform bends the frequency axis exactly, so the digital
filter's magnitude must equal
.RI 1/sqrt(1+( W / Wc )\u\s-22\fIN\fP\s+2\d)
at
.I W
= 2 tan(pi
.IR f ),
at every frequency, to rounding.
.PP
Two of the checks are about the measurement rather than the library.
The amplitude of a filtered sinusoid is measured by projecting onto that
frequency, not by taking the largest sample: at
.I f
= 0.45 the samples are 2.2 apart in a period and the crest is missed by
3%, which is a property of sampling and not of the filter.
And the windows are checked against each other for leakage rather than
against a number: rectangular must leak more than Hamming, Hamming more
than Hann, Hann more than Blackman, measured four bins from a tone that
sits half a bin off.
.SS The cross-check against circuit(2)
An RC circuit and a one-pole low-pass filter are the same object reached
from two directions, and this tree has both.
.IR Circuit (2)
integrates
.I i
=
.I C
d\fIv\fP/d\fIt\fP with the trapezoidal rule;
.IR signal (2)
maps the pole through the bilinear transform.
Two things then differ, and both are understood rather than tolerated.
.PP
The libraries put the switch at different instants:
.IR circuit (2)
has the source on at both ends of its first interval, while a filter fed
a step sees zero at the sample before the first and takes half a step.
Priming the filter's memory with the input it has been seeing makes both
solve the same problem \- and means the filter's sample 0 stands for
.I t
=
.IR h ,
not
.I t
= 0.
.PP
The pre-warping does not go away: it moves the pole by
tan(pi\fIfc\fP)/(pi\fIfc\fP).
So the check is not a tolerance plucked from the air but the
.IR rate :
halve the step and the disagreement must fall by four.
It falls by 4.007 and then 4.002.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/signaltest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR signal (2),
.IR circuit (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
The transform is checked against the direct sum only for lengths up to
128, because the direct sum is quadratic.
A bug that appeared only at some larger length would not be caught,
though the round trip is checked at every length used.
.PP
Nothing here measures speed.
The fast transform is checked to be
.IR right ,
not to be fast, and a change that made it correct and quadratic would
pass every check in this program.
.PP
The FIR stopband is checked to be below a level, not to have the shape
the window predicts, so a design that attenuated well for the wrong
reason would pass.
